<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salah Bey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1725–<a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Salah Bey ben Mostefa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salah Bey was the Ottoman bey of Constantine, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, from 1771 to 1792, under the authority of the dey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. He reformed the administration, built mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and bridges in Constantine and promoted farming in the province. Recalled and executed in 1792, he became a figure of local legend, and his death is still mourned in Constantine's songs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salah-bey.jpg" alt="Salah Bey" width="272" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadj_Ahmed_Bey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
